# KEEP-GREEN PIN — indirect-call `&`-param ABI, TYPE axis cells: **scalars**
# (`int`, `float`, `bool`) through ANNOTATED LOCAL `Callable`s.
#
# ⚠ GREEN ON ARRIVAL — AND THAT IS THE POINT. These three cells were already
# correct before the fix, but only ACCIDENTALLY: the pre-fix backends decided
# pointer-vs-value by asking "is the pointee an aggregate?", and a scalar never
# is, so the pointer happened to survive. They pin that the fix (writing the
# ABI from the callee's declared `&`) does not flip the cells the old guess got
# right by luck.
#
# RED-VERIFIED BY BREAKING THE MECHANISM (Core #12's break-and-watch, since no
# pre-fix red exists for a green-on-arrival cell):
#     break: `src/backend/c_lir/mod.rs`, `Inst::CallClosure` emit —
#            `let needs_deref = pointee.is_some();`  (i.e. deref every pointer arg)
#     result: rc 139 (SIGSEGV) on C.   restore -> rc 0, `2` / `2.000000` / `true`.
#
# LANE AXIS (self-host driver): `int &` -> rc 139, `float &` -> rc 139,
# `bool &` -> rc 139. The showcase lane is WORSE than Rust gg on these cells —
# it fails the ones Rust gg gets right. Filed separately in TODO.md.
#
# WHY known_gaps/: zero parity-corpus inflow (`runtime_parity_corpus` does not
# descend subdirectories); the LIVE test still asserts on C AND LLVM.
#
# INTENDED: `2`, `2.000000`, `true`.
void bump_int(int &n):
    n = n + 1

void bump_float(float &x):
    x = x + 1.0

void flip_bool(bool &b):
    b = not b

int main():
    Callable[void(int &)] fi = bump_int
    Callable[void(float &)] ff = bump_float
    Callable[void(bool &)] fb = flip_bool
    int n = 1
    float x = 1.0
    bool b = false
    fi(&n)
    ff(&x)
    fb(&b)
    print(n)
    print(x)
    print(b)
    return 0
